  <h1>Common Questions You May Encounter from a Skeptic</h1>

  <p class="intro">
    These are some of the most common objections to Christianity — along with clear, thoughtful ways to respond. 
    The goal is not to “win arguments”, but to think clearly, speak calmly, and point people toward truth.
  </p>

  <div class="qa">

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Am I going to hell?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> Christianity teaches that no one earns heaven — salvation is not based on being “good enough”, but on God’s grace.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Bible’s message is not that some people are good enough and others aren’t. Rather, all fall short — which is why salvation is not about performance, but about what Christ has done.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “The message of Christianity isn’t ‘be good enough’ — it’s that none of us are, which is why grace is central.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Isn’t the Bible just written by humans?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> Yes — but Christians believe those human authors were guided by God.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Bible is a collection of 66 books written over ~1,600 years by around 40 authors. Christians believe these authors wrote in their own contexts and styles, yet were guided by God.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “Christians don’t believe the Bible dropped from heaven — but that God worked through real people to communicate truth.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>How can we trust the Bible?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> The Bible is supported by early sources, multiple accounts, and strong historical transmission.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The New Testament documents were written within the lifetime of eyewitnesses. Compared to other ancient figures, the amount and closeness of source material is significant.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “If we apply the same historical standards we use for other ancient figures, the Bible holds up remarkably well.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Isn’t this just Chinese whispers over time?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> No — the ancient world relied on strong oral traditions, not casual message passing.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Stories were repeated publicly, frequently, and in communities — not whispered once between individuals. Eyewitnesses were still alive to confirm or challenge what was being said.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “It’s less like a broken telephone game and more like a group constantly repeating and verifying the same account.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Did Jesus really rise from the dead?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> The resurrection is supported by the empty tomb, eyewitness claims, and the transformation of the disciples.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The accounts report an empty tomb, multiple appearances, and a sudden shift in the disciples — from fear to bold proclamation.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “Something significant happened — the question is which explanation best fits the evidence.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Aren’t miracle claims like myths or UFO sightings?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> Not all claims are equal — historical context and evidence matter.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The resurrection claims emerged early, in a hostile environment, and were tied to specific people and places — unlike vague legends.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “We should evaluate claims based on evidence — not dismiss them simply because they are unusual.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Why are we still talking about Jesus?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> Most movements end when their leader dies — Christianity didn’t.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Christianity spread rapidly despite opposition and persecution, unlike other movements that faded.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “If nothing extraordinary happened, it’s hard to explain why this movement didn’t simply disappear.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>If God exists, why is there so much suffering?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> This question assumes objective good and evil exist.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Calling something evil assumes a real standard of good. Christianity provides a framework for this while also acknowledging suffering.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “The problem of evil is real — but it also raises the question of where our concept of ‘evil’ comes from.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Isn’t morality just a product of evolution?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> Evolution may explain behaviour — but not objective moral truth.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Evolution can describe instincts, but it cannot justify why something is truly right or wrong.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “Evolution explains feelings — not moral truth.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Does evolution disprove God?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> No — it addresses processes, not ultimate origins.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Even if evolution explains development of life, it does not explain why anything exists at all.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “Science explains how — not why.”</p>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__q"><strong>Where did everything come from?</strong></p>
    <div class="qa__a">
      <p><strong>Short Answer:</strong> The universe points beyond itself.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Questions like “Why is there something rather than nothing?” suggest a cause beyond the physical universe.</p>
      <p><strong>How to Respond:</strong> “Even if we explain how the universe began, we still need to explain why it exists.”</p>
    </div>

  </div>

